  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1143 PM CDT SAT OCT 3 2026\n\nOver the last 24-hours, there has been an unfortunate and  \nsubstantial shift in the forecast, resulting in a dramatic\ndecrease in previously forecasted rainfall amounts. Previously,\nthe disturbance moving inland from the Gulf was expected to\nbriefly move along the MS/AL state line, before taking a hard\npivot east. It was this brief period of north movement that was\ngoing to allow for plenty of interaction along the front,\nresulting in the widespread 2+ inches of rainfall. Now, this\ndisturbance is favoring a direct E/NE movement, moving along the\nFL coastline before working inland once the front has pushed\nthrough most of the state. Because of this, the main axis for\nhigher rainfall amounts continues to shift south, since the bulk\nof the tropical influence will stay south as well. At this time,\nthe main corridor for 2+ inches of rain remains along and south\nof I-85. Even this is on the northern fringe of the tropical\ndisturbance, and rainfall totals may creep down here as well.\nNorth of I-85, the bulk of the rainfall today will fall along, or\nslightly ahead of the cold front, with rainfall totals expected\nto amount to near an inch or less.\n\nAnother slight shift in the forecast are temperatures for next \nweek. The low pressure over the Midwest has also taken a more \neastward track, meaning that the trough won't dig as far south as \npreviously expected. The cold front will quickly retrograde north,\nallowing for highs to stabilize in the upper-70s east, to \nupper-80s west. Overnight lows on the other hand are still \nexpected to fall into a more seasonal feel, as cooler air filters \ndown the Appalachian Mountains, with lows generally ranging in the\n50s.\n\n/44/",
